> This can't work this way. Java writes the version number of
> its compiler into the class files and checks them when loading
> the files.

> If you really want to compile them for a earlier JVM version
> you have
> to tell it to the compiler:
> javac -target 1.5 test.java

> For even earlier versions:
> javac -target 1.4 -source 1.4 test.java

> If you're using language features that use byte codes or
> source code features that the specified version doesn't
> support, the compiler will barf.

This is something new to me.  (I last used Java professionally 6
or 7 years ago, and they claimed then that the VM was fixed for
all time.)

> > That's not necessarily true.  In fact, several experts in
> > optimization that I know claim precisely the contrary---that
> > you can get far better performance if the final optimization
> > only occurs in the actual runtime environment.  Sun's JVM
> > for Windows definitely does a better job of optimizing than
> > CygWin's g++, or from what little I've seen, VC++.

> That's an unfair comparison. The JVM with its "just in time"
> compilation feature can optimize the code dynamically *while
> it is running*.

> OTOH Stroustroups design decision "the goddam runtime
> environment must be as small as possible and that's why
> everything must be fixed at compilation" prevents such a
> dynamic nature.

That's basically what I said, no?  There are advantages for
both---if you're concerned about security and reliability, you
want to fix as much as possible in the environment you control;
for a game, it really doesn't matter, and being able to be able
to run in an environment you've never seen or heard of could
even be an advantage (unless you're offering free support, and
counting on no calls).  With regards to optimization, expert
opinions vary, but one thing is certain: most of the current
static compilers are very far from the state of the art, and
don't do nearly as good a job as they could.  (From what I've
seen with Java, this is also true for most of the JIT compilers.
But a great deal of effort has been expended on Windows, and the
Java runtime there often outperforms g++.)

Anyway, I've run a few additional tests, and come to the
conclusion that you can't use Java for portable GUI code---the
font renders work differently, and the results under Linux (64
bits) are positively unreadable, although xterm and gvim don't
have this problem.

I was somewhat surprised, in fact, to learn that Lucida
Typewriter could look so different from one application to the
next on the same machine.  I shouldn't have been, of course; I'm
already aware that it looks considerably different from one
implementation of X to the next, and of course, Swing doesn't
use the native renderer, but its own.  What I can't figure out
is why it looks so different between Linux and Windows; unless
I've misunderstood something greatly, the Swing renderer is
written in Java, and so should do exactly the same thing on each
platform.  (But there's even the difference that under Linux, the
font is called "Lucida Sans Typewriter", and under Windows
"Lucida Console".  Which means that the font renderer does
depend somehow on the native platform.)

> > Supposedly, the JVM specification hasn't changed since it was
